+## 1. The child is polynomial and its resultant degree is literally integral
+
+Moh Proposition 6.3, printed pp.197–198, gives more than a formal Puiseux
+pair. Its conclusions (1) and (2) say that \(Q'\) and every
+\(\bar T_i^\psi(\sigma)\), in particular \(P'\), lie in \(k[\gamma,\pi]\)
+and are monic in \(\pi\). Conclusion (3), after swapping Moh's displayed
+order to the \((P',Q')\) order, is
+
+\[
+ J_{X,Y}(P',Q')=cX^\ell,\qquad c\in k^*,\qquad
+ \ell=v_s-u_s-1=1.
+\]
+
+Thus \(P'_\xi=P'-\xi\) and \(Q'\) are honest polynomials in
+\(k(\xi)[X,Y]\), monic in \(Y\). Their resultant is nonzero: a common factor
+for generic \(\xi\) would give an algebraic dependence incompatible with the
+nonzero Jacobian. Consequently
+
+\[
+ I(P'_\xi,Q'):=\deg_X\operatorname{Res}_Y(P'_\xi,Q')\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}.
+\]
+
+There is no cover-degree normalization in this definition. With \(X=t^{-1}\),
+monicity gives the root-product identity
+
+\[
+ I(P'_\xi,Q')=-\operatorname{ord}_t
+ \prod_{P'_\xi(\alpha)=0}Q'(\alpha).
+\]
+
+This answers gate item (1)'s first question affirmatively. The rest of the
+gate is whether the packet sum used for that product is shifted correctly and
+is complete.
+
+## 2. Audit of the \(\ell\)-shift, statement by statement
+
+### 2.1 The only change in the chain rule
+
+Xu Lemma 4.1, printed p.4, is already stated for arbitrary
+\(J(X,Y)\). Substituting \(X=t^{-1}\) into its verbatim identity gives
+
+\[
+ Q'_Y(\alpha){dP'(\alpha)\over dt}
+ -P'_Y(\alpha){dQ'(\alpha)\over dt}
+ =-c,t^{-\ell-2}.
+\]
+
+This agrees with Moh Proposition 4.1 p.164: its printed middle Jacobian factor
+`1` is replaced by \(X^\ell=t^{-\ell}\), so every occurrence of
+\(-2+\delta\) generated by that identity becomes
+\(-2+\delta-\ell\). No other term in the chain rule moves.
+
+Xu §3 explicitly says that it does not use the Jacobian condition. Lemma 3.1,
+Proposition 3.3, and Theorem 3.4 therefore survive verbatim. Definition 4.3
+also survives verbatim.
+
+At a final major disc, the leading determinant in Xu Lemma 4.4 is nonzero, so
+
+\[
+ \lambda_P+\lambda_Q-1=\delta-2-\ell,qquad
+ \delta=\lambda_P+\lambda_Q+1+\ell<1+\ell.
+\]
+
+At a final minor disc \(\lambda_P=\lambda_Q=0\); Xu's next nonzero correction
+has exponent \(\varepsilon>0\), yielding
+\(\varepsilon-1\le\delta-2-\ell\), hence
+\(\delta>1+\ell\). These are exactly the shifted major/minor thresholds.
+
+Moh's p.169 Remark prints the shifted condition (6)* for Proposition 4.4.
+His p.171 Remark explicitly says Proposition 4.6 remains valid when (3) is
+replaced by
+
+\[
+ \lambda={-1-\ell+\delta\over n-M_r}
+ <{-1-\ell+\delta\over n-M_i}\quad(i<r).
+\]
+
+The five structural conclusions of Proposition 4.6 are unchanged: the common
+polynomial \(p\), the factor \(q\), squarefreeness of \(q\), containment of
+the roots of \(p\) in those of \(q\), and the non-power condition. On p.171
+the differential equation after substitution contains no \(\lambda\), which
+is why the factor-pattern calculus itself does not acquire a new term.
+
+### 2.2 Equations (4.3) and (4.4)
+
+For a root \(\alpha\) of \(P'_\xi\), the derivative of \(P'(\alpha)\) is
+zero. If \(\alpha\) is major, differentiation lowers the nonzero order of
+\(Q'(\alpha)\) by one, and Lemma 4.1 gives
+
+\[
+ \operatorname{ord}(P'_Y(\alpha)Q'(\alpha))=-1-\ell.
+\]
+
+If it is minor, Proposition 3.3 gives the unchanged value \(-\delta_\sigma\).
+Summing all \(m\) roots gives the exact shift
+
+\[
+ (4.3)^\ell:\quad
+ I(P'_\xi,P'_YQ')=(1+\ell)m+
+ \sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal P_m}|D^{P'_\xi}_\sigma|
+       (\delta_\sigma-1-\ell).
+\]
+
+The side bound used in (4.4) does **not** shift. The frozen producer reached
+the right bound but asserted along the way that
+\(\operatorname{ord}Q'(\beta)=0\); that equality need not hold if the
+off-\(P'_\sigma\) root of \((P'_\sigma)'\) is also a root of
+\(Q'_\sigma\). The following valuation argument repairs the proof without
+that assertion.
+
+Choose related roots \(\alpha\) of \(P'_\xi\) and \(\beta\) of \(P'_Y\) in
+a final minor disc of radius \(\delta\). Proposition 3.3 makes
+\(\operatorname{ord}(\beta-\alpha)=\delta\). For every root \(\gamma_j\) of
+\(Q'\), put \(q_j=\operatorname{ord}(\alpha-\gamma_j)\le\delta\), and let
+\(S=\{j:q_j=\delta\}\), which is nonempty. For \(j\notin S\), ultrametricity
+gives \(\operatorname{ord}(\beta-\gamma_j)=q_j\); for \(j\in S\), write that
+order as \(r_j\ge\delta\). Since \(\alpha\) is minor,
+\(\sum_jq_j=\operatorname{ord}Q'(\alpha)=0\). Every product summand in
+\(Q'_Y(\beta)\) then has order at least \(-\delta\): removing an index in
+\(S\) leaves
+
+\[
+ \sum_{j\notin S}q_j+\sum_{j\in S\setminus\{i\}}r_j
+ =-\delta+\sum_{j\in S\setminus\{i\}}(r_j-\delta)\ge-\delta,
+\]
+
+and removing an index outside \(S\) is even larger. Hence
+\(\operatorname{ord}Q'_Y(\beta)\ge-\delta\), with no use of \(J\).
+
+Lemma 4.1 at a root \(\beta\) of \(P'_Y\) now gives an exact cost
+\(1+\ell\) when \(\operatorname{ord}P'_\xi(\beta)<0\), and a cost at most
+\(\delta\) in the zero-order minor case. Since Proposition 3.3 gives
+\(|D^{P'_Y}_\sigma|=|D^{P'_\xi}_\sigma|-1\),
+
+\[
+ (4.4)^\ell:\quad
+ I(P'_Y,P'_\xi Q'_Y)\le(1+\ell)(m-1)+
+ \sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal P_m}
+ (|D^{P'_\xi}_\sigma|-1)(\delta_\sigma-1-\ell).
+\]
+
+Thus both displayed packet formulas in the frozen report have the correct
+shift, while the load-bearing side condition remains \(\ell\)-free.
+
+### 2.3 The derivative resultant and Theorem 4.7
+
+Xu's printed sentence \(I(P'_Y,Q'_Y)=0\) uses a constant Jacobian and is false
+in general for \(\ell>0\). It has a precise harmless replacement. If
+\(P'_Y,Q'_Y\) have a common specialized root above \(X=x_0\), then
+\(J(P',Q')(x_0,y_0)=0\), hence \(x_0=0\). Their leading \(Y\)-coefficients
+are the nonzero constants \(m,n\), so they have no common factor and no root
+at infinity. Therefore
+
+\[
+ \operatorname{Res}_Y(P'_Y,Q'_Y)=c_0X^k,qquad
+ k=I(P'_Y,Q'_Y)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}.
+\]
+
+Subtracting (4.4) from (4.3) gives the stronger shifted forms
+
+\[
+ \begin{aligned}
+ I(P'_\xi,P'_Y)&\le (1+\ell)(m-1)
+   +\sum_{\mathcal P_m}(|D|-1)(\delta-1-\ell)-k,\\
+ I(P'_\xi,Q')&\ge (1+\ell)
+   +\sum_{\mathcal P_m}(\delta-1-\ell)+k.
+ \end{aligned}
+\]
+
+Dropping \(k\ge0\) gives exactly the frozen report's Theorem 4.7(i),(ii)
+inequalities. Part (iii) does not survive verbatim: with no final minor roots,
+
+\[
+ I(P'_\xi,Q')=1+\ell+k,qquad
+ I(P'_\xi,P'_Y)=(1+\ell)(m-1)-k.
+\]
+
+### 2.4 Shifted Theorem 5.1 and Corollary 5.3
+
+For a complete final-major partition, Moh's printed p.171 formula gives
+
+\[
+ -\lambda_Q={n(1+\ell-\delta_\sigma)\over n-M_1}
+ ={n\over n+m}(1+\ell-\delta_\sigma),
+\]
+
+because \(M_1=-m\). Minor roots have order zero. The monic root product in
+§1 therefore proves
+
+\[
+ \boxed{I(P'_\xi,Q')=I_M^\ell(P',Q')
+ ={n\over n+m}\sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal P_M}
+ |D^{P'_\xi}_\sigma|(1+\ell-\delta_\sigma).}
+\]
+
+This is a sum over actual final discs and actual root cardinalities. Individual
+summands may be fractional; the complete sum is the resultant degree and must
+be integral. Combining it with §2.3 gives
+
+\[
+ I_M^\ell\ge I_m^\ell+k\ge I_m^\ell,qquad
+ I_m^\ell=(1+\ell)+
+ \sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal P_m}(\delta_\sigma-1-\ell).
+\]
+
+So the frozen report's shifted Theorem 5.1, new \(I_m^\ell\), and shifted
+Corollary 5.3 are correct. In particular, a minor-free \(\ell=1\) child has
+\(I_m^\ell=2\), not 1. The sealed replay explicitly used 1 as a DATA
+convention for its minor column; that convention is not the shifted theorem.
