+It must satisfy \(z+3\sum r_j=14\), have at most seven distinct roots, and
+contain multiplicity \(V'_2=3\). A multiplicity equal to
+\(P_2/Q_2=2\) is forbidden by the Proposition 4.6 differential equation:
+at such a root the leading terms cancel while its nonzero right side still has
+the original order. The possible zero exponents are
